2,\\dots ,k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThere are precisely \\(k\\) such points.\n\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines covering the remaining points  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)= i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(i\\). Since \\(i\\ge 1\\),\n\n\\[\ni\\neq 0,\\qquad i\\neq -1,\n\\]\n\nand the slope is finite; consequently \\(L_i\\) is not parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\). Hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\nThe slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct.\nBy construction each \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\(P_i\\) of (6).\n\n#### 2.4  Verification of the whole configuration  \n\nCollect all lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*   \\(|\\mathcal L|=(n-k)+k=n\\); the lines are distinct.\n*   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny.\n*   Every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs either to a horizontal line (if its\n    \\(y\\)-coordinate is \\(\\le n-k\\)) or to the unique sunny line \\(L_i\\) that\n    contains the point \\(P_i\\) in (6).  \n\nThus the two conditions of the problem are fulfilled.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* The necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\) holds for any admissible configuration.\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) an explicit configuration\n  \\(\\mathcal L\\) (as described above) satisfies the covering requirement and\n  contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\, } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps yo