uration.*  \n   Set\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n   \\]\n   \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; precisely the \\(k\\)\n   lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny, while the horizontal lines are not.\n   By construction every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) either satisfies\n   \\(b\\le n-k\\) and is covered by a horizontal line, or belongs to \\(T_k\\) and,\n   by (6), is covered by a sunny line.  Hence the covering condition is fulfilled.\n\n3. **Conclusion** – the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Together with the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) this proves that\n   the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nAny configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, therefore the number\n\\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n#### 2.1 Horizontal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence not sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on some \\(H_j\\) iff \\(b\\le n-k\\).\n\n#### 2.2 Points not covered by the horizontals  \n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\), then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n\\(a\\le k\\).  Consequently the set of uncovered points is exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\; n-k+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n\\(T_k\\) is a right‑angled integer triangle of side‑length \\(k\\); it contains\n\\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\n#### 2.3 Sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)\n\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\ny=i x+(n+1-2i).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i\\); because \\(i\\neq0\\) and \\(i\\neq-1\\), each\n\\(L_i\\) is sunny.  The slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\) are distinct, so the lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct.\n\n#### 2.4 Verification that the \\(L_i\\) cover \\(T_k\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\nb=n+1-a-t\\qquad\\text{with }t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-a\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(The existence of such a non‑negative integer \\(t\\) follows from the definition\nof \\(T_k\\); the upper bound \\(t\\le k-a\\) is equivalent to the inequality\n\\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).)\n\nConsider the line \\(L_{a}\\) (i.e. put \\(i=a\\) in (4)).  Its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=a x+(n+1-2a).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(x=a\\) we obtain the point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) on \\(L_{a}\\).  Because the slope\nof \\(L_{a}\\) is \\(a\\), decreasing the abscissa by one lowers the ordinate by\nexactly \\(a\\).  Hence for any integer \\(s\\ge0\\) the point  \n\n\\[\n(a-s,\\;n+1-a-as)\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nlies on \\(L_{a}\\).\n\nChoose \\(s=t\\) in (7).  Since \\(t\\le k-a\\), the abscissa \\(a-t\\) is a positive\ninteger not exceeding \\(k\\).  Moreover,\n\\[\nn+1-a-at = n+1-a-t = b\n\\]\nby (5).  Thus the point \\((a-t,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_{a}\\).  Because the\n\\(x\\)-coordinate of \\((a,b)\\) equals \\(a\\), we have \\(t=0\\) and the point\n\\((a,b)\\) itself is precisely the point of \\(L_{a}\\) given by (7) with\n\\(s=t\\).  Consequently \\((a,b)\\in L_{a}\\).\n\nSince the argument works for every \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\), we have proved  \n\n\\[\nT_k\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.5 The whole family of lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; the \\(k\\) lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny, while the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines are not.\nBy construction every point of \\(S_n\\) either satisfies \\(b\\le n-k\\) and is\ncovered by a horizontal line, or belongs to \\(T_k\\) and, by (8), is covered by a\nsunny line.  Hence each \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a configuration\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.  Together with the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) this shows that\nthe admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
