sunny, while the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines are not.\nBy construction every point of \\(S_n\\) either satisfies \\(b\\le n-k\\) and is\ncovered by a horizontal line, or belongs to \\(T_k\\) and, by (8), is covered by a\nsunny line.  Hence each \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a configuration\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.  Together with the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) this shows that\nthe admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
