            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nAny configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, therefore the number\n\\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n#### 2.1 Horizontal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence not sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on some \\(H_j\\) iff \\(b\\le n-k\\).\n\n#### 2.2 Uncovered points  \n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\), then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n\\(a\\le k\\).  Consequently the set of points not covered by the horizontals is\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\; n-k+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n\\(T_k\\) is a right‑angled integer triangle of side‑length \\(k\\).\n\n#### 2.3 Sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = i\\,x + \\bigl(n+1-i(i+1)\\bigr).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i\\); because \\(i\\neq0\\) and \\(i\\neq-1\\), each\n\\(L_i\\) is sunny.  The slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\) are distinct, so the lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct.\n\n#### 2.4 Verification that the \\(L_i\\) cover \\(T_k\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\nb = n+1-a - t\\qquad\\text{with }t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-a\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(The existence of such a non‑negative integer \\(t\\) follows from the definition\nof \\(T_k\\); the bound \\(t\\le k-a\\) is equivalent to the inequality\n\\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).)\n\nConsider the line \\(L_a\\) (i.e. put \\(i=a\\) in (4)).  Its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny = a\\,x + \\bigl(n+1-a(a+1)\\bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = a\\cdot a + \\bigl(n+1-a(a+1)\\bigr)=n+1-a,\n\\]\n\nso the point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  Because the slope of \\(L_a\\) is\n\\(a\\), decreasing the abscissa by one decreases the ordinate by exactly \\(a\\).\nHence for any integer \\(s\\ge0\\) the point  \n\n\\[\n(a-s,\\; n+1-a - a s)\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nbelongs to \\(L_a\\).\n\nChoose \\(s=t\\) in (7).  Since \\(t\\le k-a\\), we have \\(a-t\\ge1\\) and\n\\(a-t\\le k\\); therefore \\((a-t,\\; n+1-a - a t)\\) is a lattice point of the\nform (7).  Moreover, because \\(a\\ge1\\),\n\n\\[\nn+1-a - a t = n+1-a - t,\n\\]\n\nso the point \\((a-t,\\; n+1-a - t)\\) coincides with the original point\n\\((a,b)\\) from (5).  Consequently \\((a,b)\\in L_a\\).\n\nSince the argument works for every \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\), we have proved  \n\n\\[\nT_k\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k} L_i .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.5 The whole family of lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; precisely the \\(k\\)\nlines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny, while the horizontal lines are not.\nBy construction, every point of \\(S_n\\) either satisfies \\(b\\le n-k\\) and is\ncovered by a horizontal line, or belongs to \\(T_k\\) and, by (8), is covered by a\nsunny line.  Hence the covering condition is fulfilled.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a configuration\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering property and containing\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Together with the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) this\nshows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
