e not sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on some \\(H_j\\) iff \\(b\\le n-k\\).\n\n#### 2.2 Uncovered points  \n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\), then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n\\(a\\le k\\).  Consequently the set of points not covered by the horizontals is\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\; n-k+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n\\(T_k\\) is a right‑angled integer triangle of side length \\(k\\).\n\n#### 2.3 Sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + (n+1-2i).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(1\\); because \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(L_i\\) is\nsunny.  The intercepts \\(n+1-2i\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\)\nare distinct.\n\n#### 2.4 Verification that the \\(L_i\\) cover \\(T_k\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nc:=b-a .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom the bounds in (3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nn-2k+1\\le c\\le n+1-2a .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(n+1\\) and \\(c\\) have the same parity, hence  \n\n\\[\ni:=\\frac{n+1-c}{2}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nis an integer.  Inequality (6) gives \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).\n\nNow substitute \\(i\\) from (7) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) (4):\n\n\\[\ny = x + (n+1-2i)=x + c .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(c=b-a\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies this equation, i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).\nThus every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\), and\n\n\\[\nT_k\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.5 The whole family of lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; precisely the \\(k\\) lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny, while the horizontal lines are not.  By construction,\nevery point of \\(S_n\\) either has \\(b\\le n-k\\) and is covered by a horizontal line,\nor belongs to \\(T_k\\) and, by (8), is covered by a sunn