unny lines cannot exist, and\n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b\\le n\\), so the\npoint lies on the line \\(H_b\\).  Thus the covering condition is satisfied with\n\\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nWe need exactly one sunny line and \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nUse the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\]\n\nThese cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose \\(y\\)-coordinate is at most \\(n-2\\).\n\n*Remaining points.*  \nThe points of \\(S_n\\) not yet covered are those with \\(y=n-1\\) or \\(y=n\\).\nFrom the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain the two possibilities\n\n\\[\n(1,n-1),\\qquad (1,n).\n\\]\n\n*Sunny line.*  \nChoose the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y = x + (n-2).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L\\) is\nsunny.  The point \\((1,n-1)\\) satisfies \\(n-1 = 1 + (n-2)\\) and therefore lies\non \\(L\\).  The point \\((1,n)\\) is not needed because \\(a+b=1+n>n+1\\) when\n\\(n\\ge 3\\); indeed \\(1+n\\le n+1\\) would force \\(n\\le 0\\), contradicting\n\\(n\\ge3\\).  Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by one of the\n\\(n-1\\) horizontal lines or by the sunny line \\(L\\).\n\nThus a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(k\\le 1\\).  Constructions in Sections 2 and 3 show that both\n\\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable.  Therefore the set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO g