\n\\]\n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) is non\u2011sunny (slope \\(0\\)).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) because \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).  \n\nThus the \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\), and the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = x + (n+1-2i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\n*Each \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is sunny.*  \nThe lines are distinct because their intercepts \\(n+1-2i\\) are distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nSince \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb = (n+1-2a) + a = a + (n+1-2a).\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_a\\); i.e. \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_a\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\le n\\), the line \\(\\ell_a\\) belongs to our family.  \n\nHence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^n\\).  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are sunny, so \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions showing that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n* there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines with **no** sunny line (\\(k=0\\));\n* there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines with **all** lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).\n\nTherefore the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are always admissible.  The determination of whether any intermediate values \\(0<k<n\\) can occur remains open."
