\\) equals \\(j\\neq0,-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_{j}\\) is sunny.\n   For a column \\(x=a\\) with \\(a\\ge n-2k+1\\) we obtain the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{j,a}= \\bigl(a,\\;j(a-(n-2k+1))+1\\bigr).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a\\ge n-2k+1\\) and \\(1\\le j\\le k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\),\n\n   \\[\n   1\\le j\\bigl(a-(n-2k+1)\\bigr)+1\\le n+1-a .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The left inequality is obvious; the right one follows from\n   \\[\n   j\\bigl(a-(n-2k+1)\\bigr)+1\\le k\\bigl(a-(n-2k+1)\\bigr)+1\n   \\le k\\bigl(n-(n-2k+1)\\bigr)+1 = k(2k-1)+1\\le n+1-a,\n   \\]\n   where the last inequality uses \\(a\\ge n-2k+1\\) and the hypothesis\n   \\(k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\). Hence \\(P_{j,a}\\in T_n\\).\n\n   For a fixed column \\(a\\) the \\(k\\) points \\(\\{P_{j,a}\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\) are distinct\n   (different slopes give different \\(y\\)-coordinates). Thus each sunny line\n   contributes **exactly one** point in every column \\(C_{a}\\) with\n   \\(a\\ge n-2k+1\\).\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   - Columns \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-2k}\\) are completely covered by the vertical lines (2).  \n   - For a column \\(C_{a}\\) with \\(a\\ge n-2k+1\\) the number of points that have to be covered equals\n     \\(|C_{a}|=n+1-a\\). By the choice \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) we have\n     \\(k\\ge n+1-a\\); consequently the \\(k\\) points supplied by the sunny lines\n     \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\) are enough to cover the whole column (any surplus\n     points are simply ignored).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n   The family consists of \\(n-2k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n   i.e. exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1); for \\(k=0\\) we obtain the pure vertical family \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 together with the column‑wise counting argument yields the necessary condition (1). The explicit construction shows that (1) is also sufficient. Therefore the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely the integers \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   This completes the solution.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:x=0,\\qquad \\ell_2:y=0,\\qquad \\ell_3:x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\n*Statement.*  \nA vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); a sunny line meets each column \\(\\{x=a\\}\\) in at most one point and therefore contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a vertical line \\(x=c\\) the admissible points are \\((c,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) of them.  \nThe horizontal case is symmetric.  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\); the integer solutions with\n\\(a,b\\ge1\\) are \\((1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots\\); their number is \\(d-1\\le n\\).\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, hence it\nintersects each vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point.  Consequently it can\ncontain at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) columns, i.e. at most \\(n\\)\npoints of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nWrite \\(k\\) for the number of sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are\nnon‑sunny.\n\nConsider the columns \\(C_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\) for \\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\).\nColumn \\(C_a\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-a\\) points.\n\nA sunny line can contribute **at most one** point to a given column,\nwhereas a vertical line contributes **all** points of that column.\nHence, to cover column \\(C_a\\) we need either the vertical line \\(x=a\\) or at\nleast \\(n+1-a\\) sunny lines (one for each point of the column).\n\nIf we omit the vertical line \\(x=a\\), we must have at least \\(n+1-a\\) sunny\nlines.  Since the total number of sunny lines is \\(k\\), we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n+1-a\\qquad\\text{whenever the line }x=a\\text{ is absent}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(m\\) be the largest integer such that the vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) are present.  For the next column \\(a=m+1\\) we either keep the\nvertical line \\(x=m+1\\) or, by (6), we need \\(k\\ge n-m\\) sunny lines.\nBecause \\(k\\le n\\), the inequality \\(k\\ge n-m\\) can hold only when  \n\n\\[\nn-m\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(m\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nConsequently at least \\(\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) vertical lines are forced,\nand the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the number of columns that are\nallowed to be non‑vertical, namely  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\nSet  \n\n\\[\nv=n-2k\\qquad(\\text{note that }v\\ge\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil).\n\\]\n\n**(i) Vertical lines.**  \nTake the \\(v\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=v .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover completely the columns \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{v}\\).\n\n**(ii) Sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{j}:\\; y=j\\bigl(x-(v+1)\\bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_{j}\\) equals \\(j\\neq0,-1\\); hence each \\(\\ell_{j}\\) is sunny.\n\nFor a column \\(x=a\\) with \\(a\\ge v+1\\) the intersection point is  \n\n\\[\nP_{j,a}= \\bigl(a,\\;j(a-(v+1))+1\\bigr).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge v+1\\) and \\(1\\le j\\le k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &\\le j(a-(v+1))+1,\\\\\nj(a-(v+1))+1 &\\le k\\bigl(a-(v+1)\\bigr)+1\n               \\le k\\bigl(n-(v+1)\\bigr)+1\n               = k(2k-1)+1 \\le n+1-a .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(The last inequality uses \\(a\\ge v+1=n-2k+1\\) and the hypothesis\n\\(k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).)  Hence \\(P_{j,a}\\in T_n\\).\n\nFor a fixed column \\(a\\) the points \\(\\{P_{j,a}\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\) are distinct,\nso each sunny line contributes exactly one point in every column\n\\(C_{a}\\) with \\(a\\ge v+1\\).\n\n**(iii) Coverage.**  \n\n* Columns \\(1,\\dots ,v\\) are completely covered by the vertical lines (8).  \n* For a column \\(a\\ge v+1\\) the number of points that have to be covered is\n  \\(|C_{a}|=n+1-a\\).  Since \\(k\\ge n+1-a\\) (because \\(a\\ge v+1=n-2k+1\\)),\n  the \\(k\\) points supplied by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\)\n  are sufficient to cover the whole column.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\nThe family consists of \\(v=n-2k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and \\(k\\) sunny\nlines, i.e. exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (7); for \\(k=0\\) we\nobtain the pure vertical family \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\box