ational number whose numerator and denominator are differences of positive integers not exceeding \\(n\\).  \n\nIf the three points are not collinear with slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), then the three pairwise slopes would be distinct, contradicting the fact that a single line has a unique slope.  \nHence the only possible slopes that allow three lattice points of the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=c\\)).  \nBecause a sunny line is defined precisely as a line whose slope is none of these three values, it cannot contain three points of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus there are exactly \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) admissible points on that line.  \nThe same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \n\nIf the line has slope \\(-1\\), it is of the form \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the admissible points are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\) such points. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting argument\n\nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines among \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) and let \\(t=n-s\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(S_n\\); therefore the total number of points that can be covered by all sunny lines is at most \\(2s\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points, so the non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(nt\\) points.\n\nSince every point of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, we must have  \n\n\\[\n2s+nt\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(t=n-s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n2s+n(n-s)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n2s+ n^2-ns\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n^2+n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(2-n\\bigr)s\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n^2+n}{2}-n^2\n\\;=\\;-\\frac{n}{2},\n\\]\n\nand because \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(2-n<0\\). Multiplying the inequality by the negative number \\((2-n)\\) reverses the direction:\n\n\\[\ns\\;\\le\\;\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(s\\) is an integer and we also know that at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny, because a configuration consisting solely of sunny lines would be unable to cover the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) simultaneously (their connecting line would have slope \\(\\frac{1}{n-1}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\), contradicting Lemma\u202f1). Hence \\(t\\ge 2\\) and consequently  \n\n\\[\ns=n-t\\le n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines in a configuration satisfying the two conditions of the problem must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists. The bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) is sharp in the sense that the inequality cannot be improved by the counting argument alone; constructing explicit configurations that attain every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) remains an open part of the problem. \u220e"
