on‑sunny directions), we obtain a contradiction.\n\n*Vertical non‑sunny line.*  \nThe argument is symmetric.  \nIf the non‑sunny line is \\(x=c\\) then the set  \n\n\\[\nV_c=\\{(c,b)\\in T_n\\},\\qquad |V_c|=n+1-c\\ge 2,\n\\]\n\nmust be covered.  A sunny line can intersect the vertical line \\(x=c\\) in at most one point, so the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(V_c\\), contradicting \\(|V_c|\\ge n\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny line of slope \\(-1\\).*  \nAssume the non‑sunny line is \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nD_c=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad |D_c|=c-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause a sunny line has slope different from \\(-1\\), it can contain at most one point of \\(D_c\\); otherwise two points of \\(D_c\\) would determine a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \nThus the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(D_c\\).  \n\nIf \\(c\\le n\\) then \\(|D_c|=c-1\\le n-1\\); in order to cover the whole set \\(T_n\\) we would need at least one sunny line to contain a point outside \\(D_c\\) **and** a point of \\(D_c\\).  Such a line would have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting its sunniness.  \nIf \\(c=n+1\\) then the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) itself is non‑sunny, but the remaining points of \\(T_n\\) have sums \\(2,3,\\dots ,n\\); each of those sums defines a line of slope \\(-1\\), which would be sunny, contradicting the assumption that only one line is non‑sunny.  \n\nHence no configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line (i.e. with \\(k=n-1\\)) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions for all other values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq n-1\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  The three basic non‑sunny lines\n\nThe following three lines are non‑sunny:\n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_3:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies at least one of the equalities\n\\(b=1\\), \\(a=1\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\); consequently the three lines together cover the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  Adding sunny lines of the form \\(x+y=c\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) the line  \n\n\\[\nS_c:\\;x+y=c\n\\]\n\nis sunny, because its slope is \\(-1\\) **only** when \\(c=n+1\\).  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{S_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines and, together with \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\), also covers all points of \\(T_n\\) (the points with sum \\(c\\) lie on \\(S_c\\); the points with sum \\(n+1\\) lie on \\(L_3\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nTake the three non‑sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).  \nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct integers \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_k\\) from \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and add the sunny lines \\(S_{c_i}\\) to the family.  \nAt this stage we have \\(3+k\\) lines.  \n\nIf \\(3+k=n\\) we are done: the family consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) added ones are sunny.\n\nIf \\(3+k<n\\) we need \\(n-(3+k)\\) further lines.  \nFor each of these we add a horizontal line \\(y=t\\) with \\(t\\ge 2\\) (or a vertical line \\(x=t\\) with \\(t\\ge 2\\)); such a line is non‑sunny and does not coincide with any line already present because the ordinates (or abscissae) are chosen distinct.  \nThese extra lines do not increase the number of sunny lines, so the final family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n\\) distinct lines in total.  \nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) has ordinate at least \\(1\\) and at most \\(n\\), it lies either on one of the horizontal lines \\(y=1\\) or \\(y=t\\) (\\(t\\ge 2\\)), or on a vertical line \\(