r all points of \\(T_n\\). Repeating this replacement for any collection of distinct indices \\(s\\) yields a family of lines in which exactly the chosen indices correspond to sunny lines.\n\n3. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from the non\u2011sunny covering of step\u202f1 we can replace any subset of the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines by sunny lines, while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \n   Therefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we obtain a configuration with **exactly** \\(k\\) sunny lines (the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines being the original horizontal or the vertical line).  \n   Adding the vertical line \\(x=1\\) as a sunny line is also possible: choose a rational slope \\(\\alpha\\neq 0,-1\\) and let\n   \\[\n   S:\\; x = \\alpha y + \\beta\n   \\]\n   pass through \\((1,1)\\); this line is sunny and can replace the original vertical line. Hence the case \\(k=n\\) is also realizable.\n\nThus we have exhibited, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing **at most** \\(k\\) sunny lines. The construction can be adjusted so that the number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\) for every \\(k\\) in this range.\n\nThe remaining open question is whether any further restrictions on \\(k\\) arise from hidden geometric constraints (for example, forced coincidences when the same sunny slope is used more than once). No such obstruction has been found, but a rigorous proof that every \\(k\\) is indeed attainable **exactly** has not been completed.\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n*The detailed proof of the partial result stated above is given below.*\n\n---\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points and forms a right\u2011angled triangular lattice with vertices \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (A non\u2011sunny covering)\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: y=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\\\\\nL_n &: x=1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(b=n\\) then \\(a=1\\) and the point lies on \\(L_n\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le n-1\\) then the point lies on the horizontal line \\(L_b\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).  \nAll the lines are either horizontal (\\(L_i\\)) or vertical (\\(L_n\\)), hence none of them is sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Replacing a horizontal line by a sunny line)\n\nFix an integer \\(s\\) with \\(1\\le s\\le n-1\\).  \nChoose a rational number \\(\\alpha_s\\) such that \\(\\alpha_s\\neq 0\\) and \\(\\alpha_s\\neq -1\\).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_s:\\; y=\\alpha_s x+\\beta_s ,\n\\qquad\\text{where }\\beta_s = s-\\alpha_s .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(S_s\\) passes through the point \\((1,s)\\) and is sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSubstituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=\\alpha_s+\\beta_s = s\\), so \\((1,s)\\in S_s\\).  \nBecause \\(\\alpha_s\\neq 0\\) the line is not vertical; because \\(\\alpha_s\\neq -1\\) its slope is different from \\(-1\\); and \\(\\alpha_s\\neq 0\\) also excludes horizontality. Hence \\(S_s\\) is sunny by definition. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Preserving the covering after a replacement)\n\nLet \\(C\\) be the collection of lines from Lemma\u202f1.  \nReplace a horizontal line \\(L_s\\) (\\(1\\le s\\le n-1\\)) by the sunny line \\(S_s\\) of Lemma\u202f2, and keep all other lines unchanged.  \nThe new collection still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\neq s\\) are covered exactly as before, because the corresponding horizontal line \\(L_b\\) (or the vertical line \\(L_n\\)) is still present.  \nFor points with \\(b=s\\) we have two possibilities:\n\n* The point \\((1,s)\\) is now covered by \\(S_s\\) (by Lemma\u202f2).\n* Any other point \\((a,s)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) satisfies \\(a+s\\le n+1\\); hence \\(a\\le n+1-s\\).  \n  Since \\(s\\le n-1\\), we have \\(a\\le n\\).  \n  Such a point also lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) **only** when \\(a=1\\); otherwise it is not on any other line of the original family.  \n  However, because the slope \\(\\alpha_s\\) is rational, we can choose it so that the line \\(S_s\\) passes through **all** points \\((a,s)\\) with \\(a\\le n+1-s\\).  \n  Indeed, taking \\(\\alpha_s = \\frac{s-1}{1- (n+1-s)}\\) yields a line that contains the two extreme points \\((1,s)\\) and \\((n+1-s,s)\\); consequently it contains every intermediate lattice point on that horizontal segment.  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered after the replacement. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Realising any number of sunny lines)\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the non\u2011sunny covering of Lemma\u202f1.  \n\n* If \\(k\\le n-1\\): choose any \\(k\\) distinct indices \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\) from \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and replace the corresponding horizontal lines \\(L_{s_i}\\) by the sunny lines \\(S_{s_i}\\) constructed in Lemma\u202f2.  \n  By Lemma\u202f3 the covering property is preserved.  \n  The resulting family has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (the replaced ones) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n* If \\(k=n\\): replace **all** horizontal lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) by sunny lines as above, and also replace the vertical line \\(L_n\\) by a sunny line of the form \\(x=\\alpha y+\\beta\\) that passes through \\((1,1)\\) (choose \\(\\alpha\\neq 0,-1\\)).  \n  Again Lemma\u202f3 (applied to the vertical line) shows that the covering is maintained, and now every one of the \\(n\\) lines is sunny.\n\nIn each case the lines are distinct because the slopes (or the intercepts) are chosen differently for different indices. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion of the partial result\n\nLemma\u202f4 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) includes the whole interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nNo further restriction on \\(k\\) has been proved; determining whether additional hidden constraints exist remains an open problem. \u220e"
