            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points and forms a right‑angled triangular lattice with vertices \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (A covering without sunny lines)\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\\qquad\nL_n:\\; x=1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(b\\le n\\), either \\(b=n\\) or \\(b\\le n-1\\).\n\n* If \\(b=n\\) then \\(a=1\\) (since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_n\\).\n* If \\(b\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(L_b\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).  \nAll these lines are either horizontal or vertical, hence none of them is sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction of a sunny line through a prescribed point)\n\nFix an integer \\(s\\) with \\(1\\le s\\le n-1\\).  \nChoose a rational number  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_s:= -\\frac{s-1}{\\,n-s\\,},\n\\]\n\nwhich satisfies \\(\\alpha_s\\neq0\\) and \\(\\alpha_s\\neq-1\\) because \\(1\\le s\\le n-1\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_s:\\; y=\\alpha_s x + \\beta_s ,\\qquad\\text{where }\\beta_s:=s-\\alpha_s .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSubstituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=\\alpha_s+\\beta_s=s\\); hence \\((1,s)\\in S_s\\).  \nThe slope \\(\\alpha_s\\) is rational and different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently \\(S_s\\) is not horizontal, not vertical, and not of slope \\(-1\\). By definition such a line is sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (The line \\(S_s\\) covers the whole horizontal segment of height \\(s\\))\n\nThe set of points of \\(T_n\\) having second coordinate \\(s\\) is  \n\n\\[\nH_s:=\\{(a,s)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-s\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\alpha_s=-\\dfrac{s-1}{n-s}\\), the line \\(S_s\\) passes through the two extreme points of \\(H_s\\):\n\\[\n(1,s)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (n+1-s,s).\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\[\ny=\\alpha_s\\cdot 1+\\beta_s =\\alpha_s + (s-\\alpha_s)=s,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\ny=\\alpha_s\\,(n+1-s)+\\beta_s\n   =\\alpha_s\\,(n+1-s)+(s-\\alpha_s)\n   =\\alpha_s\\,(n-s)+s-\\alpha_s\n   =-(s-1)+s-\\alpha_s = s .\n\\]\nSince a line is determined by two distinct points, every point of the segment joining \\((1,s)\\) and \\((n+1-s,s)\\) lies on \\(S_s\\). All lattice points of \\(H_s\\) are precisely the integer points on that segment, so \\(H_s\\subseteq S_s\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Replacing a horizontal line by a sunny line preserves the covering)\n\nLet \\(C\\) be the family of lines from Lemma 1.  \nFix \\(s\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and replace the horizontal line \\(L_s\\) by the sunny line \\(S_s\\) of Lemma 2, obtaining a new family  \n\n\\[\nC' = \\bigl(C\\setminus\\{L_s\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(C'\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate is different from \\(s\\) are still covered, because the corresponding horizontal line \\(L_b\\) (or the vertical line \\(x=1\\)) remains in \\(C'\\).  \n\nFor points with second coordinate \\(s\\) we have, by Lemma 3, that the whole set \\(H_s\\) is contained in the new line \\(S_s\\). Hence these points are also covered. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Realising any prescribed number of sunny lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Case \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  \nChoose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(s\\in S\\) replace the horizontal line \\(L_s\\) by the sunny line \\(S_s\\) constructed in Lemma 2.  \nBy Lemma 4 the resulting family still covers \\(T_n\\).  \nExactly the lines indexed by \\(S\\) are sunny, so the family contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  \nProceed as above and replace **all** horizontal lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) by the corresponding sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\).  \nNow also replace the vertical line \\(L_n:x=1\\) by a sunny line of the form  \n\n\\[\nV:\\; x=\\gamma y+\\delta ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\gamma\\) is a rational number different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) (for instance \\(\\gamma=-\\frac12\\)) and \\(\\delta\\) is chosen so that \\((1,1)\\in V\\).  \nExactly the same argument as in Lemma 4 shows that the points of \\(T_n\\) that were previously covered by \\(x=1\\) remain covered by \\(V\\).  \nConsequently we obtain a covering consisting of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a suitable configuration exists. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemma 5 demonstrates that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be achieved as the exact number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinc