-------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Realising any prescribed number of sunny lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Case \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  \nChoose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(s\\in S\\) replace the horizontal line \\(L_s\\) by the sunny line \\(S_s\\) of Lemma 2.  \nBy Lemma 3 the covering is preserved.  \nExactly the lines indexed by \\(S\\) are sunny, so the resulting family has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  \nFirst perform the replacements described above for **all** \\(s=1,\\dots ,n-1\\); we now have \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and the vertical line \\(L_n:x=1\\).  \nReplace \\(L_n\\) by the sunny line \\(V\\) of Lemma 4.  \nSince \\(V\\) contains the whole set \\(V_1\\), the covering of \\(T_n\\) is still valid.  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are now sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 5 shows that each integer \\(k\\) in this set can be realised.  \nConversely, any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, so no other values of \\(k\\) are possible. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
