1}|=t-1\\) and  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\bigcup_{t=2}^{n+1} D_{t-1}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is sunny (its slope is not \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)), then for every \\(t\\) the intersection \\(L\\cap D_{t-1}\\) contains at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of the same anti‑diagonal differ by a vector \\((\\Delta a,\\Delta b)=(u,-u)\\) with \\(u\\neq0\\); the slope of the line through them is \\(\\Delta b/\\Delta a=-1\\). Since a sunny line cannot have slope \\(-1\\), it cannot contain two distinct points of the same \\(D_{t-1}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the family contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nConsider the largest anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n}\\) (size \\(n\\)). By Lemma 1 the sunny lines together contribute at most \\(k\\) points of \\(D_{n}\\); therefore the non‑sunny lines must supply at least \\(n-k\\) points of \\(D_{n}\\).\n\nA non‑sunny line can have slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).  \nA horizontal line meets \\(D_{n}\\) in at most one point (all points of \\(D_{n}\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates); the same holds for a vertical line.  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain the whole anti‑diagonal, but a line of slope \\(-1\\) can belong to **only one** anti‑diagonal because different anti‑diagonals have different constant sums \\(a+b\\).\n\nHence at most one of the non‑sunny lines can be of slope \\(-1\\); the remaining \\(n-k-1\\) non‑sunny lines are horizontal or vertical and each contributes at most one point to \\(D_{n}\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n1+(n-k-1)\\ge n-k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\text{no restriction yet}.\n\\]\n\nNow look at the second largest anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n-1}\\) (size \\(n-1\\)).  \nAgain the sunny lines give at most \\(k\\) points of \\(D_{n-1}\\).  \nThe line of slope \\(-1\\) that we used for \\(D_{n}\\) does **not** intersect \\(D_{n-1}\\) (different sums), so the remaining \\(n-k-1\\) horizontal/vertical lines must supply at least \\((n-1)-k\\) points of \\(D_{n-1}\\).  \nEach of those lines can contribute at most one point to \\(D_{n-1}\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nn-k-1\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)-k\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy (2). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix such a \\(k\\).  \n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct points \\((x_i,y_i)\\) on the largest anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n}\\) (so \\(x_i+y_i=n+1\\)).  \n   For each \\(i\\) pick a line \\(S_i\\) of slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) that passes through \\((x_i,y_i)\\) and through no other point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   This is always possible: for instance take the line with equation  \n\n   \\[\n   y-y_i = m_i (x-x_i),\\qquad m_i\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0,-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(m_i\\) is chosen so that the line does not meet any other lattice point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (there are only finitely many forbidden slopes, so a rational slope avoiding them exists).  \n   The \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny and each meets every anti‑diagonal in exactly one point (Lemma 1).\n\n2. **One line of slope \\(-1\\).**  \n   Take  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{-1}: x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   It contains all points of \\(D_{n}\\) except the \\(k\\) already covered by the sunny lines.\n\n3. **Horizontal and vertical lines for the remaining points.**  \n   After steps 1–2, every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that lies on the same vertical line as some \\((x_i,y_i)\\) is already covered (by the corresponding sunny line).  \n   The same holds for points sharing a horizontal line with a chosen point.  \n   Consequently, the only points that may still be uncovered are those whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is different from all \\(x_i\\) **and** whose \\(y\\)-coordinate is different from all \\(y_i\\).  \n\n   For each such uncovered point \\((a,b)\\) we add either the vertical line \\(x=a\\) or the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); we may decide arbitrarily, but we must avoid duplicating a line already used.  \n   Because distinct uncovered points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates and distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, the total number of added horizontal/vertical lines equals the number of uncovered points.\n\n   A direct count shows that the number of uncovered points is  \n\n   \\[\n   n-1-k .\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, on each anti‑diagonal \\(D_{t-1}\\) with \\(t\\le n\\) the sunny lines already cover exactly \\(k\\) points, and the line \\(L_{-1}\\) covers all points of \\(D_{n}\\) not among those \\(k\\); the remaining anti‑diagonals each lose at most one point per sunny line, leaving exactly \\((t-1)-k\\) points, and summing over \\(t=2,\\dots ,n\\) yields \\(n-1-k\\).)\n\n   Hence we add exactly \\(n-1-k\\) horizontal/vertical lines.\n\n4. **Counting the lines.**  \n   Total number of lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}+\n   \\underbrace{1}_{\\text{slope }-1}+\n   \\underbrace{(n-1-k)}_{\\text{horizontal/vertical}}=n .\n   \\]\n\n   All lines are distinct (the sunny lines have slopes different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); the slope \\(-1\\) line is unique; the horizontal/vertical lines are chosen with coordinates not appearing among the sunny lines).  \n\n   By construction every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nThus a configuration exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 2 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma 3 exhibits, for each integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a concrete configuration meeting all requirements.  \nTherefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely those listed above. ∎"
