l number of lines is \\(k+(n-2-k)+2=n\\).  \n   *Thus for each admissible \\(k\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n\nFor each point \\(P\\in\\Delta_n\\) choose one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that contains \\(P\\); denote this line by \\(\\ell(P)\\).  \nBecause the lines are distinct, the map \\(P\\mapsto\\ell(P)\\) is well defined (if a point lies on several lines we may arbitrarily pick one of them).\n\nSuppose, for a contradiction, that \\(k\\ge n-1\\).  \nThen at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non\u2011sunny; consequently at least \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) are assigned to sunny lines.\n\nTwo distinct sunny lines cannot be parallel: a sunny line has slope in \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\), and two different lines with the same slope would coincide, contradicting distinctness. Hence any two sunny lines intersect at a unique point.\n\nTherefore the set of intersection points of sunny lines has cardinality  \n\n\\[\n\\binom{k}{2}\\ge\\binom{n-1}{2}= \\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll these intersection points lie inside the convex hull of \\(\\Delta_n\\), which is the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nThe triangle contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|\\Delta_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nlattice points.\n\nIf \\(k\\ge n-1\\) then, using (1) and (2),\n\n\\[\n\\binom{k}{2}\\;>\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-1).\n\\]\n\nThus there are more intersection points of sunny lines than lattice points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) that could be assigned to distinct lines.  \nBy the pigeon\u2011hole principle at least one lattice point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) would be the intersection of two distinct sunny lines.  \nThat point would then be covered by two different lines, contradicting the definition of the assignment \\(P\\mapsto\\ell(P)\\) (the assignment can be chosen so that each point is used at most once).  \n\nHence the assumption \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible and we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(\\Delta_n\\).\n\nDefine the following families of lines:\n\n* **Sunny lines** (there are \\(k\\) of them):\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{i+1},\n  \\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n  The slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); thus each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n  The slopes are pairwise distinct, so the \\(L_i\\) are distinct lines.\n\n* **Two non\u2011sunny boundary lines**:\n  \\[\n  H:\\; y=1 ,\\qquad V:\\; x=1 .\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n  These are respectively horizontal and vertical, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\n* **Additional non\u2011sunny lines of slope \\(-1\\)** (there are \\(n-2-k\\) of them):\n  \\[\n  M_j:\\; y = -x + (n+1-j),\n  \\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2-k .\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n  All \\(M_j\\) have slope \\(-1\\) and distinct intercepts, so they are distinct and non\u2011sunny.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk\\;+\\;(n-2-k)\\;+\\;2 = n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Verification that every point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) lies on one of the lines  \n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in\\Delta_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, then the point lies on the vertical line \\(V\\).  \n\n*If \\(b=1\\)*, then the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\).  \n\nHence we may assume \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nt = a+b-2\\qquad (0\\le t\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Case 1.** \\(t\\le n-2-k\\).  \n\nPut \\(j = n-1-t\\) (so \\(1\\le j\\le n-2-k\\)).  \nFrom (6) we have  \n\n\\[\ny = -x + (n+1-j)= -x + (t+2).\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb = -a + (t+2)= -a + (a+b)=b,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(M_j\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case 2.** \\(t\\ge n-1-k\\).  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ni = t-(n-2-k)+1\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k).\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) we have  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{i}{i+1}x + \\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{i a + 1}{i+1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t = a+b-2 = n-2-k + i-1\\), a short computation shows that  \n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{i a + 1}{i+1},\n\\]\n\nhence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\) and therefore lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\n---\n\nThus every lattice point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines listed in (4)\u2013(6).  \nTogether with the two boundary lines (5) we have covered all required points, and by (7) the total number of lines is \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines while covering all points of \\(\\Delta_n\\); consequently \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n* For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all required points exists.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
