angle …”\n\n*Correct* (the convex hull of \\(\\Delta_n\\) is that triangle, and any line joining two points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) stays inside it; however, the intersection of two arbitrary sunny lines need not be a point of \\(\\Delta_n\\), only inside the triangle).\n\n#### 2.5. Comparison with the number of lattice points\n\n> “The triangle contains exactly \\(|\\Delta_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) lattice points.”  \n> “If \\(k\\ge n-1\\) then … \\(\\binom{k}{2} > \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-1).\\)”\n\n*Correct* arithmetic.\n\n#### 2.6. Pigeon‑hole step\n\n> “Thus there are more intersection points of sunny lines than lattice points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) that could be assigned to distinct lines. By the pigeon‑hole principle at least one lattice point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) would be the intersection of two distinct sunny lines. That point would then be covered by two different lines, contradicting the definition of the assignment …”\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nThe argument assumes that each intersection point of two sunny lines must coincide with a *lattice* point of \\(\\Delta_n\\). In reality, the \\(\\binom{k}{2}\\) intersection points are generally **real** points; many of them are not integer lattice points. The pigeon‑hole principle can only be applied to a finite set of *objects* placed into *boxes*; here the “boxes” are the lattice points of \\(\\Delta_n\\), but the “objects” (intersection points) are not guaranteed to lie in those boxes. Consequently the conclusion that some lattice point is the intersection of two sunny lines is unfounded. This invalidates the derived bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\nBecause this error breaks the logical chain, any subsequent statements that rely on the bound are unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n#### 3.1. Definition of the lines\n\n*Sunny lines*  \n\\(L_i: y = \\frac{i}{i+1}x + \\frac{1}{i+1},\\; i=1,\\dots ,k\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny boundary lines*  \n\\(H: y=1,\\; V: x=1\\).\n\n*Additional non‑sunny lines of slope \\(-1\\)*  \n\\(M_j: y = -x + (n+1-j),\\; j=1,\\dots ,n-2-k\\).\n\n*Correct* that the slopes are as claimed and that the total number of lines is \\(k+(n-2-k)+2=n\\) (equation (7)).\n\n#### 3.2. Claim that every \\((a,b)\\in\\Delta_n\\) lies on one of the lines\n\nThe proof splits into cases based on \\(t=a+b-2\\).\n\n##### Case 1 (points on an \\(M_j\\))\n\n> “If \\(t\\le n-2-k\\) … set \\(j=n-1-t\\). Then from (6) we have \\(y = -x + (t+2)\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(b = -a + (a+b)=b\\).”\n\n*Correct* – for any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(t\\le n-2-k\\) the point indeed satisfies the equation of \\(M_j\\). This part of the covering works.\n\n##### Case 2 (points on an \\(L_i\\))\n\n> “If \\(t\\ge n-1-k\\) … define \\(i = t-(n-2-k)+1\\). … Substituting \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y = \\frac{i a + 1}{i+1}\\). Because \\(t = a+b-2 = n-2-k + i-1\\), a short computation shows that \\(b = \\frac{i a + 1}{i+1}\\).”\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nThe algebraic claim that \\(b = \\frac{i a + 1}{i+1}\\) follows from the definition of \\(i\\) is **false**. A concrete counter‑example disproves it:\n\n- Take \\(n=5,\\;k=2\\). Then \\(n-2-k = 1\\).  \n- Choose the point \\((a,b) = (2,2)\\). Here \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b=4\\le n+1=6\\), so \\((2,2)\\in\\Delta_5\\).  \n- Compute \\(t = a+b-2 = 2\\). Since \\(t\\ge n-1-k = 5-1-2 =2\\), we are in Case 2.  \n- According to the construction, \\(i = t-(n-2-k)+1 = 2-1+1 = 2\\).  \n- The line \\(L_2\\) is \\(y = \\frac{2}{3}x + \\frac{1}{3}\\). Plugging \\(x=2\\) gives \\(y = \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}= \\frac{5}{3}\\neq 2\\).  \n\nThus \\((2,2)\\) is **not** on any \\(L_i\\). The same failure occurs for many other points (e.g. \\((3,2)\\) when admissible). Hence the case analysis does not guarantee coverage of all points with \\(a,b\\ge2\\).\n\nBecause the construction does not actually cover all points of \\(\\Delta_n\\), the claim “Thus every lattice point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines listed in (4)–(6)” is false.\n\n---\n\