stinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least two vertical lines.  \nThen there exists a sunny line \\(L\\) such that replacing **one** of the vertical lines of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) by \\(L\\) produces another family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPick a vertical line \\(V:x=c\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with abscissa \\(c\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the other lines of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) are not vertical, each of these points is covered either by \\(V\\) itself or by some non‑vertical line.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nb_0:=\\min\\{\\,b\\mid (c,b)\\in T_n\\text{ and }(c,b)\\text{ is **not** covered by any line of }\\mathcal{F}\\setminus\\{V\\}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nSuch a \\(b_0\\) exists: the point \\((c,\\,n+1-c)\\) is covered by \\(V\\) and, if it were also covered by another line, that line would be vertical, contradicting the assumption that only the lines of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) are vertical.\n\nChoose a rational number \\(m\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\nm\\neq 0,\\;m\\neq -1,\n\\]\n\nand also such that the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y-b_0 = m\\,(x-c)\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ndoes not coincide with any line already present in \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) (this is possible because there are infinitely many admissible slopes).  \nEquation (2) defines a line that passes through the single point \\((c,b_0)\\) of the column \\(x=c\\); because \\(m\\neq 0\\) it meets the column only at that point, so no other point of the column loses its covering when \\(V\\) is removed.\n\nNow replace \\(V\\) by \\(L\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with abscissa \\(c\\) are still covered: the point \\((c,b_0)\\) is now covered by \\(L\\), and every other point of the column remains covered by the same non‑vertical line that covered it before (or, if it was covered only by \\(V\\), it is covered by \\(L\\) because \\(L\\) meets the column at exactly \\((c,b_0)\\) and the column contains no other uncovered point).  \nAll points with abscissa different from \\(c\\) are untouched.  \nThus the new family still satisfies the covering condition, contains exactly one more sunny line than before, and all its lines are distinct. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive construction\n\nStart with the family \\(\\mathcal{F}_0=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\) (all vertical, \\(k=0\\)).  \nAssume we have built a family \\(\\mathcal{F}_m\\) with exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines, \\(0\\le m\\le n-3\\), and at least two vertical lines remaining.  \nApply Lemma 3.1 to \\(\\mathcal{F}_m\\); we obtain a new family \\(\\mathcal{F}_{m+1}\\) with \\(m+1\\) sunny lines.  \nBecause each application removes one vertical line, after \\(m\\) steps there are \\(n-m\\) vertical lines left; the lemma can be applied as long as \\(n-m\\ge 2\\), i.e. while \\(m\\le n-2\\).\n\nConsequently for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nEquation (1) shows that any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe constructions above demonstrate that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is indeed realizable.  \n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
