ints of the diagonal  \n   \\(\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\); the line \\(L_{1}\\) contains all points with abscissa \\(1\\).  \n   Consequently every point of the required set is already covered by either \\(L_{0}\\) or \\(L_{1}\\) **or** lies in a column \\(x=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) and ordinate \\(b\\le n-c\\).\n\n   *Replacing vertical columns by sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) we have the column  \n\n   \\[\n   C_{c}=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-c\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The highest point of \\(C_{c}\\) is \\((c,n-c)\\), which lies on \\(L_{0}\\).  \n   Choose a rational number \\(\\alpha_{c}\\) satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_{c}\\neq 0,\\qquad \\alpha_{c}\\neq -1,\n   \\]\n\n   and also \\(\\alpha_{c}\\neq \\alpha_{c'}\\) for distinct columns that we shall turn into sunny lines (this is possible because there are infinitely many admissible slopes).  \n   Define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{c}:\\; y-(n-c)=\\alpha_{c}\\bigl(x-c\\bigr). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_{c}\\) passes through the point \\((c,n-c)\\) of the diagonal and, because its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\), it meets the column \\(x=c\\) **only** at that point.  \n   Hence after we delete the vertical line \\(x=c\\) and insert \\(S_{c}\\) the following holds:\n\n   * the point \\((c,n-c)\\) remains covered (now by \\(S_{c}\\));\n   * every other point of the column \\(C_{c}\\) is still covered by the line \\(L_{1}\\) when \\(c=1\\) or by the still‑present vertical line \\(x=c\\) when we have not yet replaced that column.\n\n   Consequently, as long as at least two of the original vertical columns are left untouched, the set of lines continues to cover **all** points of the required region.\n\n   *Inductive replacement.*  \n   Start with the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}_{0}= \\{L_{0},L_{1}\\}\\cup\\{x=c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which contains exactly two non‑sunny lines and \\(n-2\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines.  \n   For any integer \\(m\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le n-2\\) we perform the following inductive procedure:\n\n   *Assume* we have already constructed a family \\(\\mathcal{F}_{m}\\) that contains the two fixed non‑sunny lines \\(L_{0},L_{1}\\), exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines of the form \\(S_{c}\\), and \\(n-2-m\\) untouched vertical lines \\(x=c\\).  \n   Because \\(n-2-m\\ge 2\\) when \\(m\\le n-4\\), there are at least two columns still represented by a vertical line.  \n   Choose one such column, say \\(x=c_{0}\\), and replace the vertical line \\(x=c_{0}\\) by the sunny line \\(S_{c_{0}}\\) defined in (2) with a slope \\(\\alpha_{c_{0}}\\) not used before.  \n   By the discussion above, the new family \\(\\mathcal{F}_{m+1}\\) still covers every point of the required set, now contains \\(m+1\\) sunny lines, and still has at least one vertical line left (until \\(m=n-2\\)).  \n\n   Repeating this replacement \\(k\\) times yields a family \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the two fixed ones plus \\(n-2-k\\) remaining vertical lines).  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \n   The explicit construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \n\n   Hence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
