d) or to the sunny line that we placed through it (if its column was replaced).  \n\n   Consequently, for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\nSince the construction works for every admissible \\(k\\), no other restrictions on \\(k\\) exist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\); geometrically it is the set of integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\ncover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\)). Hence the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) the point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\n\\]\n\nbelongs to \\(T_n\\) and is not collinear with any other point of \\(T_n\\) on a line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(n+1-i\\ge1\\), the point satisfies the defining inequalities of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf a line through \\(P_i\\) had slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) and contained another point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\), then\n\\[\n\\frac{b-(n+1-i)}{a-i}=m\\neq -1,0,\\infty .\n\\]\nSince both coordinates are integers, this would force \\(a-i\\) and \\(b-(n+1-i)\\) to be non‑zero integers with the same ratio for two distinct pairs, which is impossible because the set \\(T_n\\) contains at most one point with a given \\(x\\)-coordinate on the diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\