bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=n\\) lines in total.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\). Hence either \\(a\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) (so the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(b\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) (so the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)). Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Replacing a forbidden line by a sunny line).\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and suppose that \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or horizontal (\\(y=c\\)). Then there exists a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) such that\n\n* \\(\\ell'\\) contains exactly the same points of \\(T_n\\) as \\(\\ell\\);\n* \\(\\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\\) still consists of distinct lines and still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(\\ell\\) is vertical, \\(\\ell:x=c\\). The set of points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\nS_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose a rational number \\(\\alpha=\\frac pq\\) with \\(q>0\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) such that \\(q\\) divides every integer in the interval \\([1,n+1-c]\\). For instance, take \\(q=n+1\\) and \\(p=1\\); then \\(\\alpha=1/(n+1)\\).  \n\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell' :\\; y-b = \\alpha (x-c)\n\\]\n\npassing through the first point \\((c,1)\\in S_c\\). For any integer \\(t\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n(c+t,\\;1+\\alpha t)=\\bigl(c+t,\\;1+\\tfrac{p}{q}t\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(q\\mid t\\) for all \\(t\\) that keep the second coordinate integral, the points of \\(\\ell'\\) with integer coordinates are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(c+q\\ell,\\;1+p\\ell)\\mid \\ell\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;1\\le 1+p\\ell\\le n+1-c\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are coprime, the map \\(\\ell\\mapsto (c+q\\ell,1+p\\ell)\\) is a bijection between the integer points of \\(\\ell'\\) and the set \\(S_c\\). Hence \\(\\ell'\\) contains exactly the same integer points of \\(T_n\\) as \\(\\ell\\).  \n\nThe slope \\(\\alpha\\) is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\) (because \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(q\\neq p\\)), so \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny.  \n\nThe same construction works when \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, by swapping the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Increasing the number of sunny lines).\n\nStarting from the family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) of Lemma 1, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe proceed by induction on \\(k\\).\n\n*Base case \\(k=0\\).*  \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) itself satisfies the claim.\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines has been constructed. Because \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) contains \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, at least one of them is vertical or horizontal (otherwise all non‑sunny lines would have slope \\(-1\\), but there are at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) distinct lines of slope \\(-1\\) that can pass through points of \\(T_n\\); a simple counting argument shows this is impossible for \\(n\\ge3\\)). Choose such a line \\(\\ell\\). By Lemma 2 we can replace \\(\\ell\\) with a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) without altering the set of covered points. The resulting family \\(\\mathcal L_{k+1}\\) has exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines and still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem (Partial answer to the original problem).\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n\n* every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply Lemma 3 with the given \\(n\\) and the desired \\(k\\). The family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) supplied by the lemma meets both required conditions. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Remark.**  \nThe construction above uses at most one line of each forbidden direction (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)). If a configuration were required to contain several lines of the same forbidden direction, the replacement technique of Lemma 2 may no longer be applicable, and the existence of such configurations for arbitrary \\(k\\) remains open. This constitutes the main gap in obtaining a complete classification of all admissible values of \\(k\\)."
