ost one point, while any non‑sunny line can meet **both** diagonals in at most one point total (a vertical line meets only \\(D_n^{(1)}\\), a horizontal line only \\(D_n^{(2)}\\), a slope‑\\(-1\\) line meets none).  \n   The two diagonals together contain \\(2(n-1)\\) points. Counting the maximum number of points that can be covered gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot 2+(n-k)\\cdot 1\\ge 2(n-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{Lemma 3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction (Lemma 2).**  \n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   m=n-2k\\qquad(m\\ge1).\n   \\]\n\n   *Non‑sunny part.*  \n   Take the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) take the line of slope \\(+1\\)\n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\;x-y=m+j .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{V_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}\\cup\\{S_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le k\\}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines; the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) are sunny, the \\(m\\) vertical lines are non‑sunny.\n\n   **Coverage proof.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   *Case 1: \\(a\\le m\\).*  \n   Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   *Case 2: \\(a>m\\).*  \n   Because \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b\\le n+1=m+2k+1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   a-b = (a+b)-2b \\le (m+2k+1)-2\\cdot1 = m+2k-1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover \\(a>b\\); otherwise \\(a\\le b\\le n+1-a\\le m\\), contradicting \\(a>m\\).  \n   Thus \\(1\\le a-b\\le m+2k-1\\).\n\n   The integers  \n\n   \\[\n   m+1,\\;m+2,\\;\\dots ,\\;m+k\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   form a block of \\(k\\) consecutive numbers contained in the interval \\([1,m+2k-1]\\).  \n   From (3) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b = (a-b)+2b \\le (m+2k-1)+2b .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(b\\ge1\\), the inequality \\(a+b\\le m+2k+1\\) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   a-b\\ge m+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Combining with (4) we get  \n\n   \\[\n   m+1\\le a-b\\le m+k .\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore there exists a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) such that \\(a-b=m+j\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (1) for that \\(j\\) and lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\), and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) can be realised, while Lemma 3 proves that larger \\(k\\) are impossible. Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set that must be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).  \nThe problem asks for \\(n\\) distinct lines that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 3  \nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the two “near‑diagonals’’  \n\n\\[\nD^{(1)}=\\{(i,n-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\},\\qquad   \nD^{(2)}=\\{(i+1,n-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth sets are contained in \\(T_n\\) and together contain exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) points.\n\n*Intersection properties.*\n\n* A sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently it meets each of the sets \\(D^{(1)}\\) and \\(D^{(2)}\\) in **at most one** point (otherwise two points of the same set would determine a line of slope \\(-1\\) or \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\), contradicting the definition of sunny).\n\n* A non‑sunny line can be\n  * vertical – it can meet \\(D^{(1)}\\) in at most one point and never meets \\(D^{(2)}\\);\n  * horizontal – it can meet \\(D^{(2)}\\) in at most one point and never meets \\(D^{(1)}\\);\n  * slope \\(-1\\) – it meets **none** of the two sets, because points of either set satisfy \\(x+y=n\\) or \\(x+y=n+1\\), not a constant.\n\nHence each non‑sunny line contributes at most **one** point to the union \\(D^{(1)}\\cup D^{(2)}\\), while each sunny line contributes at most **two** points (one from each diagonal).\n\nIf the family contains \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, the total number of points of \\(D^{(1)}\\cup D^{(2)}\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2k+(n-k)=n+k .\n\\]\n\nSince all \\(2(n-1)\\) points of the two diagonals must be covered, we obtain  \n\n\\[\nn+k\\ge 2(n-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\ge n-2(n-1)=2-n .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\ge0\\) this inequality is always true; the non‑trivial information comes from the fact that **at most one** line of slope \\(-1\\) can appear in the family (otherwise two such lines would coincide). If a slope‑\\(-1\\) line is present, it is non‑sunny and contributes **zero** points to the two diagonals, so the previous count improves to  \n\n\\[\n2k+(n-k-1)\\ge 2(n-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration satisfies the claimed bound. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nm=n-2k\\qquad(m\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\underbrace{\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,m\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical, non‑sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x-y=m+j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\,\\}}_{\\text{slope }+1,\\ \\text{sunny}} .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines; the second block consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof of coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le m\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which belongs to the first block.\n\n*Assume \\(a>m\\).*  \nFrom the defining inequality of \\(T_n\\),\n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1=m+2k+1 . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a>b\\) (otherwise \\(a\\le b\\le n+1-a\\le m\\), contradicting \\(a>m\\)), we have \\(b\\ge1\\) and\n\n\\[\na-b = (a+b)-2b \\le (m+2k+1)-2 = m+2k-1 . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, (7) gives  \n\n\\[\na-b = (a+b)-2b \\ge (m+2k+1)-2(m)=m+2k+1-2m = m+2k+1-2m .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\ge1\\) this lower bound simplifies to \\(a-b\\ge m+1\\).  \nCombining with (8) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nm+1\\le a-b\\le m+k . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus there exists a unique integer \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) such that \\(a-b=m+j\\).  \nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x-y=m+j\\) and lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\) of the second block.\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\), and exactly the \\(k\\) lines of slope \\(+1\\) are sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Final conclusion  \n\nLemma 2 shows that for each integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma 3 proves that no configuration can have more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
