ey already lie on \\(L\\) which has one of those directions), the slope of \\(L_1\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence \\(L_1\\) is sunny.\n\nLet \\(L_2\\) be the line through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\); this line has the same direction as \\(L\\) (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)), so \\(L_2\\) is non‑sunny.\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) that lay on \\(L\\) is either \\(P_1\\) or \\(P_2\\) or \\(P_3\\) or lies between \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_3\\) on \\(L\\).\nAll points between \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) belong to \\(L_2\\); all points between \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\) belong to \\(L_1\\) (because a line is determined by any two of its points).  \nThus the union \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\) contains exactly the same subset of \\(T_n\\) as the original line \\(L\\).\n\nFinally, \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are distinct from each other (they have different slopes) and, because their slopes differ from those of all lines already in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (which are either vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)), they cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Obtaining any prescribed number \\(k\\)\n\nWe start from the covering \\(\\mathcal L_0=\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) given by Lemma 1; it consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers \\(T_n\\).\n\nAssume we have already constructed a family \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) (\\(0\\le t<k\\)) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.\nBecause at most two lines of \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) can have fewer than three points of \\(T_n\\), there exists a non‑sunny line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L_t\\) that contains at least three points of \\(T_n\\).\nApply Lemma 2 to \\(L\\) (with \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal L_t\\)).\nWe replace \\(L\\) by the two new lines \\(L_1\\) (sunny) and \\(L_2\\) (non‑sunny) obtained in the lemma, and then delete one of the remaining lines of \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) (any line that is not needed for covering) so that the total number of lines stays equal to \\(n\\).\nBecause \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\) covers exactly the points formerly covered by \\(L\\), the covering property is preserved.\nMoreover the number of sunny lines has increased by one (the newly introduced \\(L_1\\)).\nThus we obtain a new family \\(\\mathcal L_{t+1}\\) with \\(t+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nRepeating this procedure \\(k\\) times yields a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every point of \\(T_n\\);\n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the construction works for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Non‑existence of other values\n\nWe use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).\nThe construction above shows that every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is achievable.\nHence no other non‑negative integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final answer\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
