s \\((a,b)\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\) and therefore lies on the diagonal line of \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\). ∎\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) is a valid configuration with \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Adding a sunny line without destroying the covering  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_{n}\\) and contains \\(s\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le s<n\\)).  \nWe describe a transformation that produces a new family \\(\\mathcal F'\\) with \\(s+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Selection of two points.*  \nChoose two points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\in T_{n}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\na_{1}\\neq a_{2},\\qquad b_{1}\\neq b_{2},\\qquad a_{1}+b_{1}\\neq a_{2}+b_{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge 3\\), the set \\(T_{n}\\) contains at least three points, and a pair satisfying (1) can be found (for instance \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,3)\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\); for \\(n=3\\) the pair \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) works).\n\n*The sunny line.*  \nLet \\(L^{\\mathrm{s}}\\) be the unique line through the two chosen points.  \nCondition (1) guarantees that the slope of \\(L^{\\mathrm{s}}\\) is neither \\(0\\) (different \\(y\\)-coordinates), nor \\(\\infty\\) (different \\(x\\)-coordinates), nor \\(-1\\) (different sums \\(a+b\\)).  \nHence \\(L^{\\mathrm{s}}\\) is sunny.\n\n*Removal of an old line.*  \nAmong the \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) at most two can pass through a given point; consequently at most four lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) pass through the two selected points.  \nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\), there exists at least one line \\(L^{\\mathrm{r}}\\in\\mathcal F\\) that contains **neither** of the two points.  \nDelete \\(L^{\\mathrm{r}}\\) from \\(\\mathcal F\\) and insert \\(L^{\\mathrm{s}}\\).  \nDenote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal F'\\).\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n*Covering.*  \nThe two points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\) are now covered by \\(L^{\\mathrm{s}}\\).  \nEvery other point of \\(T_{n}\\) was covered by some line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) that is still present in \\(\\mathcal F'\\) (we removed only a line that did not contain the two points).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal F'\\) still covers all of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \n\\(L^{\\mathrm{s}}\\) is sunny, while all other lines of \\(\\mathcal F'\\) are exactly the lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) except \\(L^{\\mathrm{r}}\\).  \nTherefore the number of sunny lines has increased by one: \\(|\\{ \\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal F'\\}| = s+1\\).\n\n*Distinctness.*  \n\\(L^{\\mathrm{s}}\\) is different from every line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) because its slope is sunny whereas all lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) may be sunny or non‑sunny, but the particular line \\(L^{\\mathrm{s}}\\) does not coincide with any line that avoids the two points (otherwise it would pass through at least one of them).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal F'\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nThus the transformation is valid.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)  \n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) (which has \\(0\\) sunny lines), apply the transformation of Section 2 repeatedly.  \nAfter the first application we obtain a family with \\(1\\) sunny line, after the second a family with \\(2\\) sunny lines, and so on.  \nBecause each step keeps the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\), we can perform the step at most \\(n\\) times.  \n\nConsequently, for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Upper bound  \n\nSince only \\(n\\) lines are used, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions exists are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
