 except \\(L^{\\mathrm{r}}\\).  \nTherefore the number of sunny lines has increased by one: \\(|\\{ \\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal F'\\}| = s+1\\).\n\n*Distinctness.*  \n\\(L^{\\mathrm{s}}\\) is different from every line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) because its slope is sunny whereas all lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) may be sunny or non‑sunny, but the particular line \\(L^{\\mathrm{s}}\\) does not coincide with any line that avoids the two points (otherwise it would pass through at least one of them).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal F'\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nThus the transformation is valid.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)  \n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) (which has \\(0\\) sunny lines), apply the transformation of Section 2 repeatedly.  \nAfter the first application we obtain a family with \\(1\\) sunny line, after the second a family with \\(2\\) sunny lines, and so on.  \nBecause each step keeps the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\), we can perform the step at most \\(n\\) times.  \n\nConsequently, for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Upper bound  \n\nSince only \\(n\\) lines are used, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions exists are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct